  "dom_id": "step-q133923",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the number of years passed since $2000$ and the population of a particular town. Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho ≈0.9,$ which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho ≈0.9,$ there is a *strong positive* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/aac79b793bde599899ff7ff8ca880e198bd5481178c2c5a0e2ac96acf223bdaa.png)",
